sections:
- intended use
- how to use
- available parameters
- warnings that can be raised by this map



intended use
------------

This map is intended for use with amide (~N(D)C(=O)~) (smiles notation) groups, specifically those that are found in the protein backbone. In other words, it looks for the amide units formed through linking amino acids.

The goal is to select the correct coupling map to be used for calculating the coupling between two amide groups. That coupling map will then handle the rest - this map cannot calculate couplings itself.

how to use
----------

This map is not designed and intended for stand-alone use, yet should always be requested directly. It should be used together with the 'ProteinAmide_something'-series maps, and with the DipDip map that comes with GMAP.
The reason for this, is that GMAP cannot distinguish between groups any further than knowing what singles map belongs to them. So, for example, there is no way to instruct GMAP to use one coupling map when two AmideBB are connected through bond, and another coupling map otherwise. This map takes that upon itself. The user tells GMAP to use this map for coupling AmideBB and/or AmideSC groups, and can give this map instructions on what map should be used when.

Please note, this map is specifically designed for coupling AmideI singles map. It has been tested to work with the AmideBB and AmideSC singles maps, so we recommend to only couple those types of oscillators with this map. Any MD-system requirements for those maps automatically apply here as well.


Available parameters
--------------------

When using this map, the following parameters become available to use in any input parameter source (default file, input file, command line):

ProteinAmide.coupling_choice
    no shorthand
    options: None, DipDip, TDCTasumi, TDCKrimm, TCC (default = TDCTasumi)

    What coupling method should be applied to couple two groups that are not direct neighbours. If one or both of them is a sidechain amide, it will automatically be assigned this choice.

ProteinAmide.NN_coupling_choice
    no shorthand
    options: None, DipDip, TDCTasumi, TDCKrimm, TCC, Tasumi, GLDP (default = GLDP)

    What coupling method should be applied to couple two groups that are direct neighbours. If one or both of the groups is a sidechain amide, this option will never be used, but assigned the choice for coupling_choice instead. 

ProteinAmide.legacy_mode
    no shorthand
    options: None, AIM, AmideImaps (default = None)

    Whether to recreate the results as produced by the precursors of this package. These are the differences:
    - (method selection) AmideImaps always assigned GLDP for two backbone neighbours that had non-bordering residue numbers - i.e. opposite 'ends' of a cyclic chain. AIM (and GMAP default) apply the requested nearest-neighbour choice to this case.
    - (method selection) AmideImaps always assigned a TDC method to the coupling between two sidechains, instead of also allowing for TCC coupling. So, if TCC coupling is requested during this legacy mode, any two sidechains will be coupled by TDCTasumi instead.
    - (GLDP model only) When there are two prolines back to back, what map is used changes a little bit between modes. AmideImaps assigned a Gly-Pro map for those cases, while AIM and None assign a Pro-Gly map.


warnings that can be raised by this map
---------------------------------------

map_ProteinAmide_GLDP_1
    This error is raised when any of the neighbour influence maps has the wrong dimensions, or anything else is wrong with the file.

map_ProteinAmide_GLDP_2
    While determining the chirality of residues between two amide oscillators, a residue was found that is not a GLY residue, but neither has a CB atom. Therefore, this cannot be a cannonical aminoacid, or you are using an unexpected nomenclature. If you are sure you are applying the right map to the right system, please get in touch with the map developers to solve this issue.